// First real-hardware reachability check for RP1's USB host controllers
// (GitHub issue #161's own future-work note in
// examples/common_rpi5/AGENTS.md: "RP1's Ethernet path... is its own
// future subject if pursued" -- USB is the analogous case, needed for
// examples/el0_shell_rpi5, which is blocked on it).
//
// Per the official Raspberry Pi RP1 Peripherals datasheet
// (datasheets.raspberrypi.com/rp1/rp1-peripherals.pdf, Chapter 5): "The
// USB Host subsystem is based on Synopsys IP dwc_usb3, v3.30b. There are
// two identical USB3.0 xHCI Host Controllers conforming to the
// Extensible Host Controller Interface Specification v1.2." This is a
// REAL XHCI controller, not the simpler DWC2 OTG controller RPi5's own
// AGENTS.md previously (before this research) speculated about -- a
// materially bigger driver task than DWC2 would have been. This example
// deliberately does nothing risky yet: it only READS the standard XHCI
// Capability register block (CAPLENGTH/HCIVERSION/HCSPARAMS1-3/
// HCCPARAMS1), the same "prove basic reachability before touching
// anything stateful" step examples/rp1_pcie_smoke itself started from
// for RP1's PCIe link.
//
// Register base addresses (datasheet Table 85, "5.1. Register base
// addresses"): usbhost0 (main XHCI MMIO, AXI3 bus, Atomic Access: N --
// plain reads, no atomic-access aliasing needed) lives at RP1-internal
// address 0x40200000; usbhost1 at 0x40300000. RP1-internal addresses map
// to CPU physical addresses via the SAME offset examples/common_rpi5/
// uart.tkb's own rp1_uart0 (RP1-internal 0x40030000, datasheet Table 22)
// already proved: CPU_PHYS = RP1_INTERNAL + 0x1EC0000000. So usbhost0's
// Capability registers are reachable at CPU physical 0x1F00200000 and
// usbhost1's at 0x1F00300000 -- both already covered by
// examples/common_rpi5/mmu.S's existing L1[124..127] 4GB RP1 outbound
// window (no new MMU/PCIe work needed), and PCIe/RP1 BAR bring-up
// already runs unconditionally for every RPi5 kernel via platform_init
// (examples/common_rpi5/pcie.tkb), the same reason rp1_uart0 already
// works in every other example without an explicit init call here.
//
// Checkpoint instrumentation (same technique as rp1_pcie_smoke, GitHub
// issue #161): a global written immediately before each read so a hang's
// last-reached step is SWD-readable after power-cycle recovery -- RP1's
// own "usbhost0_cfg"/"usbhost1_cfg" register blocks (datasheet Table 85,
// Atomic Access: Y, addresses not yet explored here) may gate clock/
// power/reset state this smoke test does not yet configure, so a hang or
// bus-timeout-pattern (0xFFFFFFFF) read is a real, expected possible
// outcome, not assumed impossible.
//
// Step 2 (2026-07-26 follow-up, after Capability-register reachability
// was confirmed): reads USBSTS and every PORTSC the CONTROLLER ITSELF
// reports existing (HCSPARAMS1 bits[31:24], MaxPorts -- read on real
// hardware as 3 per controller here, not guessed), via the STANDARD XHCI
// Operational register layout (Intel's public XHCI spec, not anything
// RP1-specific or requiring the still-undocumented usbhost*_cfg block --
// this datasheet's own USB chapter has no per-bit register list for
// usbhost*_cfg at all, unlike its GPIO/PWM chapters, so this step
// deliberately routes around that gap rather than guessing at it).
// Operational registers start at Capability base + CAPLENGTH (0x20, read
// in Step 1 above -- both controllers report the same value). USBSTS is
// at Operational+0x04; PORTSC for 1-based port n is at
// Operational+0x400+(n-1)*0x10. Still entirely read-only: USBSTS.CNR
// (Controller Not Ready, bit 11) is checked and reported explicitly,
// since per the XHCI spec every OTHER Operational register (except
// PAGESIZE) is not guaranteed valid while it is set -- if CNR is set,
// the PORTSC values read here would need to be treated as provisional,
// not a real bring-up bug.
//
// Step 2 real-hardware results (both controllers, confirmed reproducible
// across two runs): usbhost0 USBSTS=0x18 (CNR=0, HCH=0 -- the controller
// is NOT halted, i.e. already running: firmware/TF-A left USBCMD.RS set
// before handing off, this bare-metal payload never touched it). port 1
// and 2 both read 0x2a0 (PP=1 powered, PLS=5 RxDetect, Speed=0 --
// ordinary idle/nothing-connected state). Port 3 reads 0x21203 (CCS=1
// CONNECTED, PED=1 ENABLED, PLS=0 U0 fully-operational link, PP=1,
// Speed=4) -- something is live and already enumerated-by-firmware on
// usbhost0 port 3. usbhost1 shows all three ports idle (0x2a0) and
// USBSTS=0 (no EINT/PCD pending). Open question this raises, NOT yet
// answered: the datasheet says each controller has only 2 PHYs (one
// USB2, one USB3), so why does HCSPARAMS1 report 3 ports? Step 3 below
// investigates via the standard xHCI Extended Capabilities list instead
// of guessing.
//
// Step 3 (2026-07-26 follow-up): walks the xHCI Extended Capabilities
// linked list (pointed to by HCCPARAMS1's xECP field, bits[31:16], a
// DWORD offset from the Capability register base) looking for
// Capability ID 2 ("Supported Protocol Capability" per the public xHCI
// spec), which maps a compatible port RANGE to a USB protocol
// major/minor revision -- the standard way a real driver resolves
// "port 3 in HCSPARAMS1's flat MaxPorts count is actually protocol X"
// without guessing. Still entirely read-only. Bounded to 8 entries
// (this repo's own established defensive-loop-cap convention, e.g.
// examples/el0_elf_load's own MAX_PHDRS) -- a real xHCI implementation
// normally has only a handful of extended capabilities, so 8 is
// generous headroom, not a tight guess.

use "examples/common_rpi5/dma_asm_extern.tkb";
let mut usb_smoke_checkpoint: u32 = 0;
let mut usb_smoke_host0_cap0: u32 = 0xAAAAAAAA;
let mut usb_smoke_host0_hcsparams1: u32 = 0xAAAAAAAA;
let mut usb_smoke_host0_hcsparams2: u32 = 0xAAAAAAAA;
let mut usb_smoke_host0_hcsparams3: u32 = 0xAAAAAAAA;
let mut usb_smoke_host0_hccparams1: u32 = 0xAAAAAAAA;
let mut usb_smoke_host1_cap0: u32 = 0xAAAAAAAA;
let mut usb_smoke_host1_hcsparams1: u32 = 0xAAAAAAAA;
let mut usb_smoke_host0_usbsts: u32 = 0xAAAAAAAA;
let mut usb_smoke_host0_portsc1: u32 = 0xAAAAAAAA;
let mut usb_smoke_host0_portsc2: u32 = 0xAAAAAAAA;
let mut usb_smoke_host0_portsc3: u32 = 0xAAAAAAAA;
let mut usb_smoke_host1_usbsts: u32 = 0xAAAAAAAA;
let mut usb_smoke_host1_portsc1: u32 = 0xAAAAAAAA;
let mut usb_smoke_host1_portsc2: u32 = 0xAAAAAAAA;
let mut usb_smoke_host1_portsc3: u32 = 0xAAAAAAAA;
// Byte offset (from the Capability register base) of the extended
// capability entry about to be read -- doubles as the checkpoint value
// for this loop, so a hang's exact failing address is SWD-readable
// directly (not just "somewhere in iteration N").
let mut usb_smoke_ecp_offset: u32 = 0xAAAAAAAA;
let mut usb_smoke_ecp_dword: u32 = 0xAAAAAAAA;

// -- Step 6 real DMA buffers (GitHub issue #67 follow-up) -------------------
// Device Context Base Address Array: 65 x 8-byte pointers -- entry 0 is
// the Scratchpad Buffer Array pointer (required whenever HCSPARAMS2's
// Max Scratchpad Buffers field is nonzero -- read on real hardware as 2
// here, see this file's own header comment), entries 1..64 are per-slot
// Device Context pointers (only slot 1 is ever populated by this
// example, since only one Enable Slot is issued). 64-byte aligned per
// the xHCI spec's own DCBAAP requirement.
let mut usb_smoke_dcbaa: [u8; 65 * 8] align(64);
// Scratchpad Buffer Array (2 pointers, per HCSPARAMS2) plus the two real
// 4KB buffers it points to. Zero-filled (BSS) is a valid initial
// scratchpad buffer content -- the controller treats these as opaque
// save/restore space, never interpreted by software.
let mut usb_smoke_scratchpad_array: [u8; 2 * 8] align(64);
let mut usb_smoke_scratchpad_buf0: [u8; 4096] align(4096);
let mut usb_smoke_scratchpad_buf1: [u8; 4096] align(4096);
// Command Ring: 4 TRBs (16 bytes each) -- TRB[0] holds whatever command
// is currently being issued (Enable Slot for this step), TRB[1..2] are
// reserved for a future step, TRB[3] is the mandatory Link TRB (Toggle
// Cycle set) wrapping back to TRB[0].
const CMD_RING_TRBS: usize = 16;
let mut usb_smoke_cmd_ring: [u8; CMD_RING_TRBS * 16] align(64);
// Event Ring: 16 TRBs -- one segment, no Link TRB (the Event Ring uses
// the ERST to define segment boundaries; the controller wraps within a
// segment on its own, toggling its Producer Cycle State).
const EVT_RING_TRBS: usize = 16;
let mut usb_smoke_evt_ring: [u8; EVT_RING_TRBS * 16] align(64);
// Event Ring Segment Table: one 16-byte entry (base address + size).
let mut usb_smoke_erst: [u8; 16] align(64);

// -- Step 7 buffers (Address Device, GitHub issue #67 follow-up) ------------
// HCCPARAMS1 (0x0240fe6d, read in Step 1) has CSZ (bit2) = 1: this
// controller uses 64-BYTE contexts, not the 32-byte default -- every
// context below is 64-byte strided even though only the first 32 bytes
// of each carry real fields (the rest is reserved/vendor padding per the
// xHCI spec's own CSZ=1 shape).
//
// Input Context: Input Control Context (offset 0) + Slot Context (offset
// 64) + one Endpoint Context per DCI (DCI n at offset (n+1)*64). Address
// Device needs only DCI 1 (EP0), but Configure Endpoint adds the bulk
// endpoints, so both buffers are allocated at their full spec size
// (33 and 32 contexts) rather than being resized per milestone.
const CTX_STRIDE: usize = 64;
const INPUT_CTX_SIZE: usize = 2112;  // 33 * CTX_STRIDE
const DEVICE_CTX_SIZE: usize = 2048; // 32 * CTX_STRIDE
let mut usb_smoke_input_ctx: [u8; INPUT_CTX_SIZE] align(64);
// Device Context: Slot Context (offset 0) + Endpoint Context per DCI
// (DCI n at offset n*64) -- OUTPUT, written BY the controller once
// Address Device completes. DCBAA[slot_id] must point here BEFORE
// issuing the command (zeroed BSS is the correct "nothing written yet"
// initial state).
let mut usb_smoke_device_ctx: [u8; DEVICE_CTX_SIZE] align(64);
// Transfer Rings for the Mass Storage bulk endpoints, allocated up front
// so Configure Endpoint can point each Endpoint Context's TR Dequeue
// Pointer at one. Same trailing-Link-TRB shape as the EP0 ring.
const BULK_RING_TRBS: usize = 16;
let mut usb_smoke_bulk_in_ring: [u8; BULK_RING_TRBS * 16] align(64);
let mut usb_smoke_bulk_out_ring: [u8; BULK_RING_TRBS * 16] align(64);
// Endpoint 0's own Transfer Ring -- same 4-TRB-with-trailing-Link-TRB
// shape as the Command Ring, but this ring carries Setup/Data/Status
// Stage TRBs for control transfers, not commands, and its doorbell is
// per-slot-per-endpoint (Doorbell Array index = slot ID), not doorbell 0.
// 16 TRBs: 15 usable plus a trailing Link TRB. Each control transfer
// costs exactly 3 TRBs, so 15 divides evenly and a transfer can never
// straddle the Link TRB -- but usb_ctrl_xfer() still checks, so changing
// this constant stays safe.
const EP0_RING_TRBS: usize = 16;
let mut usb_smoke_ep0_ring: [u8; EP0_RING_TRBS * 16] align(64);
// Data Stage landing buffer for control transfers. The controller DMAs
// descriptor bytes straight into this, so it needs the same cache
// maintenance discipline as the rings: clean before handing it over,
// invalidate before reading the result back.
let mut usb_smoke_ctrl_buf: [u8; 256] align(64);

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Shared XHCI driver state, in globals so usb_ctrl_xfer() below can be a
// plain function instead of taking a dozen parameters. Every one of
// these is initialized during the bring-up sequence in app_main before
// any transfer is attempted.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
let mut usb_xhci_slot_id: usize = 0;
// Producer side of the EP0 Transfer Ring: index of the next free TRB,
// and the Cycle bit value a newly written TRB must carry. The cycle
// state flips every time the producer follows the Link TRB back to the
// start, which is what tells the controller which entries are new.
let mut usb_xhci_ep0_enq: usize = 0;
let mut usb_xhci_ep0_cycle: u32 = 1;
// Consumer side of the Event Ring: index of the next entry not yet
// consumed, and the Cycle bit an entry must carry to count as valid.
// Same toggle-on-wrap rule, in the opposite direction.
let mut usb_xhci_evt_idx: usize = 0;
let mut usb_xhci_evt_cycle: u32 = 1;
// Out-of-band results from the last usb_ctrl_xfer() call, since a
// function returns only one value.
let mut usb_xhci_last_residual: u32 = 0;
let mut usb_xhci_last_evt_dw3: u32 = 0;

// What the Configuration Descriptor walk found: the configuration to
// select, and the Mass Storage interface's Bulk-Only Transport endpoint
// pair. bMaxBurst comes from the SuperSpeed Endpoint Companion
// descriptor and is zero-based (0 = one packet per burst).
let mut usb_msc_config_value: u32 = 0;
let mut usb_msc_interface_num: u32 = 0;
let mut usb_msc_bulk_in_ep: u32 = 0;
let mut usb_msc_bulk_in_mps: u32 = 0;
let mut usb_msc_bulk_in_burst: u32 = 0;
let mut usb_msc_bulk_out_ep: u32 = 0;
let mut usb_msc_bulk_out_mps: u32 = 0;
let mut usb_msc_bulk_out_burst: u32 = 0;

// Consume one Event Ring entry of the requested TRB Type, skipping (and
// reporting) any other event type that arrives first -- port status
// changes in particular are asynchronous and interleave freely.
// Advances ERDP past everything consumed, which the controller requires
// to keep delivering events. Returns the completion code (DWORD2 bits
// 31:24), or 0 if no matching event turned up.
fn usb_await_event(want_type: u32, timeout_ms: usize) -> u32 {
    let evt_ring_base: usize = (usb_smoke_evt_ring as *u8) as usize;
    let evt_ring_dma: usize = evt_ring_base + RP1_DMA_PCI_OFFSET;
    let ir0_base: usize = 0x1F00200000 + 0x460 + 0x20;

    for scan: usize in 0..<8 {
        let off: usize = usb_xhci_evt_idx * 16;
        let mut seen: bool = false;
        let mut dw3: u32 = 0;
        for i: usize in 0..<timeout_ms {
            rpi5_dcache_invalidate_range(evt_ring_base + off, 16);
            let dw3_ptr: *u32 = (evt_ring_base + off + 12) as *u32;
            let d3: u32 = *dw3_ptr;
            if ((d3 & 1) == usb_xhci_evt_cycle) {
                seen = true;
                dw3 = d3;
                break;
            }
            delay_us(1000);
        }
        if (seen == false) {
            return 0;
        }

        let dw2_ptr: *u32 = (evt_ring_base + off + 8) as *u32;
        let dw2: u32 = *dw2_ptr;
        let trb_type: u32 = (dw3 >> 10) & 0x3F;

        // Advance the consumer position first, so ERDP is always
        // correct no matter which branch is taken below.
        usb_xhci_evt_idx = usb_xhci_evt_idx + 1;
        if (usb_xhci_evt_idx >= EVT_RING_TRBS) {
            usb_xhci_evt_idx = 0;
            usb_xhci_evt_cycle = 1 - usb_xhci_evt_cycle;
        }
        let erdp_lo_ptr: *io u32 = (ir0_base + 0x18) as *io u32;
        *erdp_lo_ptr = ((evt_ring_dma + usb_xhci_evt_idx * 16) & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
        let erdp_hi_ptr: *io u32 = (ir0_base + 0x1C) as *io u32;
        *erdp_hi_ptr = (((evt_ring_dma + usb_xhci_evt_idx * 16) >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;

        if (trb_type == want_type) {
            usb_xhci_last_residual = dw2 & 0xFFFFFF;
            usb_xhci_last_evt_dw3 = dw3;
            return (dw2 >> 24) & 0xFF;
        }

        uart_puts("  (skipped event trb_type=");
        uart_print_hex(trb_type as i32);
        uart_puts(")\r\n");
    }
    return 0;
}

// One USB control transfer over EP0: Setup Stage + optional Data Stage +
// Status Stage, one doorbell ring, one Transfer Event. Shape follows
// U-Boot's xhci_ctrl_tx(). Pass w_length=0 for a no-data request, in
// which case buf_dma is ignored. Returns the Transfer Event's completion
// code (1 = Success, 0 = no event); the residual byte count lands in
// usb_xhci_last_residual.
fn usb_ctrl_xfer(bm_request_type: u32, b_request: u32, w_value: u32,
                 w_index: u32, w_length: u32, buf_dma: usize,
                 dir_in: bool) -> u32 {
    let ep0_ring_base: usize = (usb_smoke_ep0_ring as *u8) as usize;
    let has_data: bool = w_length > 0;

    // A transfer must not straddle the Link TRB. With 3 TRBs per
    // transfer and 15 usable entries this never triggers today, but
    // wrapping early keeps the ring correct if either number changes.
    let need: usize = 3;
    if (usb_xhci_ep0_enq + need > EP0_RING_TRBS - 1) {
        usb_xhci_ep0_enq = 0;
        usb_xhci_ep0_cycle = 1 - usb_xhci_ep0_cycle;
    }
    let first_off: usize = usb_xhci_ep0_enq * 16;
    let cyc: u32 = usb_xhci_ep0_cycle;

    // Setup Stage TRB (Type 2). DWORD2 is always 8, the size of a USB
    // setup packet. IDT (bit6) says the 8 setup bytes are immediate
    // data held in DWORD0/1 rather than fetched from memory. TRT
    // (bits 17:16): 3 = IN data stage follows, 2 = OUT, 0 = no data.
    let mut trt: u32 = 0;
    if (has_data) {
        if (dir_in) { trt = 3; } else { trt = 2; }
    }
    let s_dw0_ptr: *u32 = (ep0_ring_base + first_off) as *u32;
    *s_dw0_ptr = bm_request_type | (b_request << 8) | (w_value << 16);
    let s_dw1_ptr: *u32 = (ep0_ring_base + first_off + 4) as *u32;
    *s_dw1_ptr = w_index | (w_length << 16);
    let s_dw2_ptr: *u32 = (ep0_ring_base + first_off + 8) as *u32;
    *s_dw2_ptr = 8;
    let s_dw3_ptr: *u32 = (ep0_ring_base + first_off + 12) as *u32;
    *s_dw3_ptr = cyc | (1 << 6) | (2 << 10) | (trt << 16);

    let mut off: usize = first_off + 16;

    // Data Stage TRB (Type 3). DIR (bit16) = 1 for IN.
    if (has_data) {
        let mut dir_bit: u32 = 0;
        if (dir_in) { dir_bit = 1; }
        let d_dw0_ptr: *u32 = (ep0_ring_base + off) as *u32;
        *d_dw0_ptr = (buf_dma & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
        let d_dw1_ptr: *u32 = (ep0_ring_base + off + 4) as *u32;
        *d_dw1_ptr = ((buf_dma >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
        let d_dw2_ptr: *u32 = (ep0_ring_base + off + 8) as *u32;
        *d_dw2_ptr = w_length;
        let d_dw3_ptr: *u32 = (ep0_ring_base + off + 12) as *u32;
        *d_dw3_ptr = cyc | (3 << 10) | (dir_bit << 16);
        off = off + 16;
    }

    // Status Stage TRB (Type 4). No buffer, no length. The status
    // handshake runs opposite to the data stage, so an IN data stage
    // takes a DIR=0 status stage and vice versa; a no-data request
    // uses DIR=1. IOC (bit5) goes here -- this is the TRB that must
    // raise the Transfer Event.
    let mut st_dir: u32 = 1;
    if (has_data) {
        if (dir_in) { st_dir = 0; }
    }
    let t_dw0_ptr: *u32 = (ep0_ring_base + off) as *u32;
    *t_dw0_ptr = 0;
    let t_dw1_ptr: *u32 = (ep0_ring_base + off + 4) as *u32;
    *t_dw1_ptr = 0;
    let t_dw2_ptr: *u32 = (ep0_ring_base + off + 8) as *u32;
    *t_dw2_ptr = 0;
    let t_dw3_ptr: *u32 = (ep0_ring_base + off + 12) as *u32;
    *t_dw3_ptr = cyc | (1 << 5) | (4 << 10) | (st_dir << 16);

    usb_xhci_ep0_enq = (off + 16) / 16;
    rpi5_dcache_clean_range(ep0_ring_base, EP0_RING_TRBS * 16);

    // Doorbell array is indexed by slot ID, 4 bytes per entry (slot 0
    // being the Command doorbell). DB Target = DCI 1 for EP0.
    let db_ptr: *io u32 = (0x1F00200500 + usb_xhci_slot_id * 4) as *io u32;
    *db_ptr = 1;

    // Transfer Event is TRB Type 32.
    return usb_await_event(32, 1000);
}

fn app_main() {
    uart_puts("rp1_usb_smoke\n");

    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 1;
    // Offset 0x00: CAPLENGTH (bits 7:0) | reserved (bits 15:8) |
    // HCIVERSION (bits 31:16, BCD -- e.g. 0x0110 for xHCI v1.10). Read as
    // one 32-bit word (no *io u16 precedent exists yet in this repo) --
    // the standard way real XHCI drivers read this pair together.
    let host0_cap0_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200000;
    usb_smoke_host0_cap0 = *host0_cap0_ptr;
    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 2;

    let host0_hcsparams1_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200004;
    usb_smoke_host0_hcsparams1 = *host0_hcsparams1_ptr;
    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 3;

    let host0_hcsparams2_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200008;
    usb_smoke_host0_hcsparams2 = *host0_hcsparams2_ptr;
    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 4;

    let host0_hcsparams3_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F0020000C;
    usb_smoke_host0_hcsparams3 = *host0_hcsparams3_ptr;
    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 5;

    let host0_hccparams1_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200010;
    usb_smoke_host0_hccparams1 = *host0_hccparams1_ptr;
    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 6;

    uart_puts("usbhost0 CAPLENGTH|HCIVERSION: ");
    uart_print_hex(usb_smoke_host0_cap0 as i32);
    uart_puts("\r\nusbhost0 HCSPARAMS1: ");
    uart_print_hex(usb_smoke_host0_hcsparams1 as i32);
    uart_puts("\r\nusbhost0 HCSPARAMS2: ");
    uart_print_hex(usb_smoke_host0_hcsparams2 as i32);
    uart_puts("\r\nusbhost0 HCSPARAMS3: ");
    uart_print_hex(usb_smoke_host0_hcsparams3 as i32);
    uart_puts("\r\nusbhost0 HCCPARAMS1: ");
    uart_print_hex(usb_smoke_host0_hccparams1 as i32);
    uart_puts("\r\n");

    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 7;
    let host1_cap0_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00300000;
    usb_smoke_host1_cap0 = *host1_cap0_ptr;
    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 8;

    let host1_hcsparams1_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00300004;
    usb_smoke_host1_hcsparams1 = *host1_hcsparams1_ptr;
    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 9;

    uart_puts("usbhost1 CAPLENGTH|HCIVERSION: ");
    uart_print_hex(usb_smoke_host1_cap0 as i32);
    uart_puts("\r\nusbhost1 HCSPARAMS1: ");
    uart_print_hex(usb_smoke_host1_hcsparams1 as i32);
    uart_puts("\r\n");

    // Step 2: standard XHCI Operational registers, Capability base +
    // CAPLENGTH (0x20, confirmed identical for both controllers above).
    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 10;
    let host0_usbsts_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200024;
    usb_smoke_host0_usbsts = *host0_usbsts_ptr;
    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 11;

    let host0_portsc1_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200420;
    usb_smoke_host0_portsc1 = *host0_portsc1_ptr;
    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 12;

    let host0_portsc2_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200430;
    usb_smoke_host0_portsc2 = *host0_portsc2_ptr;
    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 13;

    let host0_portsc3_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200440;
    usb_smoke_host0_portsc3 = *host0_portsc3_ptr;
    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 14;

    uart_puts("usbhost0 USBSTS: ");
    uart_print_hex(usb_smoke_host0_usbsts as i32);
    uart_puts(" CNR=");
    uart_print(((usb_smoke_host0_usbsts >> 11) & 1) as usize);
    uart_puts("\r\nusbhost0 PORTSC1: ");
    uart_print_hex(usb_smoke_host0_portsc1 as i32);
    uart_puts("\r\nusbhost0 PORTSC2: ");
    uart_print_hex(usb_smoke_host0_portsc2 as i32);
    uart_puts("\r\nusbhost0 PORTSC3: ");
    uart_print_hex(usb_smoke_host0_portsc3 as i32);
    uart_puts("\r\n");

    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 15;
    let host1_usbsts_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00300024;
    usb_smoke_host1_usbsts = *host1_usbsts_ptr;
    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 16;

    let host1_portsc1_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00300420;
    usb_smoke_host1_portsc1 = *host1_portsc1_ptr;
    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 17;

    let host1_portsc2_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00300430;
    usb_smoke_host1_portsc2 = *host1_portsc2_ptr;
    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 18;

    let host1_portsc3_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00300440;
    usb_smoke_host1_portsc3 = *host1_portsc3_ptr;
    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 19;

    uart_puts("usbhost1 USBSTS: ");
    uart_print_hex(usb_smoke_host1_usbsts as i32);
    uart_puts(" CNR=");
    uart_print(((usb_smoke_host1_usbsts >> 11) & 1) as usize);
    uart_puts("\r\nusbhost1 PORTSC1: ");
    uart_print_hex(usb_smoke_host1_portsc1 as i32);
    uart_puts("\r\nusbhost1 PORTSC2: ");
    uart_print_hex(usb_smoke_host1_portsc2 as i32);
    uart_puts("\r\nusbhost1 PORTSC3: ");
    uart_print_hex(usb_smoke_host1_portsc3 as i32);
    uart_puts("\r\n");

    // Step 3: walk usbhost0's xHCI Extended Capabilities list. xECP
    // (HCCPARAMS1 bits[31:16]) is a DWORD offset from the Capability
    // base to the first entry; each entry's low byte is its Capability
    // ID, the next byte is a DWORD offset to the NEXT entry (0 = end of
    // list) -- both relative to the CURRENT entry's own address, per the
    // xHCI spec (not relative to the Capability base again).
    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 21;
    let xecp_dwords: usize = ((usb_smoke_host0_hccparams1 >> 16) & 0xFFFF) as usize;
    let mut ecp_addr: usize = 0x1F00200000 + xecp_dwords * 4;
    uart_puts("usbhost0 first ext cap addr: ");
    uart_print_hex((ecp_addr - 0x1F00200000) as i32);
    uart_puts("\r\n");

    if (xecp_dwords != 0) {
        for i: usize in 0..<8 {
            usb_smoke_ecp_offset = (ecp_addr - 0x1F00200000) as u32;
            usb_smoke_checkpoint = 22;
            let ecp_ptr: *io u32 = ecp_addr as *io u32;
            usb_smoke_ecp_dword = *ecp_ptr;
            usb_smoke_checkpoint = 23;

            let cap_id: u32 = usb_smoke_ecp_dword & 0xFF;
            let next_dwords: u32 = (usb_smoke_ecp_dword >> 8) & 0xFF;

            uart_puts("ext cap @+");
            uart_print_hex(usb_smoke_ecp_offset as i32);
            uart_puts(" id=");
            uart_print_hex(cap_id as i32);
            uart_puts(" raw=");
            uart_print_hex(usb_smoke_ecp_dword as i32);
            uart_puts("\r\n");

            if (cap_id == 1) {
                // USB Legacy Support Capability (USBLEGSUP): bit16 = HC
                // BIOS Owned Semaphore, bit24 = HC OS Owned Semaphore.
                // Neither set means no firmware/BIOS-handoff ownership
                // claim was ever made through this mechanism -- on a PC
                // platform this register is how BIOS hands the
                // controller to an OS driver; RP1/TF-A apparently just
                // leaves the controller running without engaging it.
                let bios_owned: u32 = (usb_smoke_ecp_dword >> 16) & 1;
                let os_owned: u32 = (usb_smoke_ecp_dword >> 24) & 1;
                uart_puts("  legacy support: bios_owned=");
                uart_print(bios_owned as usize);
                uart_puts(" os_owned=");
                uart_print(os_owned as usize);
                uart_puts("\r\n");
            }

            if (cap_id == 2) {
                // Supported Protocol Capability: DWORD1 = name string
                // (ASCII "USB "), DWORD2 bits[7:0]=Compatible Port
                // Offset (1-based), bits[15:8]=Compatible Port Count.
                // Major revision is this entry's own DWORD0 bits[31:24].
                let dw2_ptr: *io u32 = (ecp_addr + 8) as *io u32;
                let dw2: u32 = *dw2_ptr;
                let port_offset: u32 = dw2 & 0xFF;
                let port_count: u32 = (dw2 >> 8) & 0xFF;
                let major_rev: u32 = (usb_smoke_ecp_dword >> 24) & 0xFF;
                uart_puts("  supported protocol: major_rev=");
                uart_print_hex(major_rev as i32);
                uart_puts(" port_offset=");
                uart_print(port_offset as usize);
                uart_puts(" port_count=");
                uart_print(port_count as usize);
                uart_puts("\r\n");
            }

            if (next_dwords == 0) {
                break;
            }
            ecp_addr = ecp_addr + (next_dwords as usize) * 4;
        }
    }

    // Step 4: DBOFF (Doorbell array offset, Capability+0x14) and RTSOFF
    // (Runtime register space offset, Capability+0x18) -- both standard
    // XHCI Capability registers, still read-only. Needed later to ring
    // the Command doorbell and configure the Event Ring's interrupter
    // registers, before any real Enable Slot command can be issued.
    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 31;
    let host0_dboff_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200014;
    let host0_dboff: u32 = *host0_dboff_ptr;
    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 32;
    let host0_rtsoff_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200018;
    let host0_rtsoff: u32 = *host0_rtsoff_ptr;
    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 33;

    uart_puts("usbhost0 DBOFF: ");
    uart_print_hex((host0_dboff & 0xFFFFFFFC) as i32);
    uart_puts("\r\nusbhost0 RTSOFF: ");
    uart_print_hex((host0_rtsoff & 0xFFFFFFE0) as i32);
    uart_puts("\r\n");

    // Step 5 (2026-07-26 follow-up): the FIRST real WRITE against this
    // hardware in the whole USB bring-up effort. Per the XHCI spec,
    // DCBAAP/CRCR (needed for a real Enable Slot command) may only be
    // written while the controller is HALTED (USBCMD.RS=0) -- but Step 2
    // found it already RUNNING (USBSTS.HCH=0, left that way by
    // firmware/TF-A at handoff, per USBLEGSUP's own bios_owned=0/
    // os_owned=0 finding in Step 4: no ownership handoff was ever
    // negotiated). So this step performs ONLY a clean halt: read
    // USBCMD, clear bit 0 (RS) via read-modify-write, then POLL
    // USBSTS.HCH (bit 0) until it reads 1 -- confirming the controller
    // genuinely stopped, not just that the write was accepted. Bounded
    // to 100 iterations x 1ms (100ms total, generous headroom over the
    // xHCI spec's own expected halt latency) via delay_us
    // (examples/common_rpi5/pcie.tkb, already linked into this kernel
    // for platform_init's own PCIe bring-up) -- a real timed wait, not a
    // busy-spin iteration count (the same lesson issue #169 already
    // taught this codebase: an iteration-count loop's real duration
    // depends on cache/clock state, a timed wait does not). Does NOT
    // yet touch DCBAAP/CRCR/CONFIG -- those are still a separate, later
    // step, only safe to attempt once this halt is confirmed.
    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 40;
    let host0_usbcmd_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200020;
    let usbcmd_before: u32 = *host0_usbcmd_ptr;
    uart_puts("usbhost0 USBCMD before halt: ");
    uart_print_hex(usbcmd_before as i32);
    uart_puts("\r\n");

    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 41;
    let usbcmd_halted: u32 = usbcmd_before & 0xFFFFFFFE;
    *host0_usbcmd_ptr = usbcmd_halted;
    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 42;

    let mut halt_confirmed: bool = false;
    let mut halt_polls: usize = 0;
    for i: usize in 0..<100 {
        usb_smoke_checkpoint = 43;
        let usbsts_poll_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200024;
        let usbsts_poll: u32 = *usbsts_poll_ptr;
        halt_polls = i + 1;
        if ((usbsts_poll & 1) == 1) {
            halt_confirmed = true;
            break;
        }
        delay_us(1000);
    }
    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 44;

    let usbcmd_after_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200020;
    let usbcmd_after: u32 = *usbcmd_after_ptr;
    let usbsts_after_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200024;
    let usbsts_after: u32 = *usbsts_after_ptr;
    uart_puts("usbhost0 USBCMD after write: ");
    uart_print_hex(usbcmd_after as i32);
    uart_puts("\r\nusbhost0 USBSTS after halt attempt: ");
    uart_print_hex(usbsts_after as i32);
    uart_puts("\r\nhalt confirmed: ");
    if (halt_confirmed) {
        uart_puts("yes, polls=");
        uart_print(halt_polls);
    } else {
        uart_puts("NO (timed out)");
    }
    uart_puts("\r\n");

    // Real Host Controller Reset (USBCMD.HCRST, bit1) -- a plain RS=0/
    // RS=1 cycle (Step 5 above) stops the ring doorbell/scheduling but
    // does NOT necessarily discard the controller's own INTERNAL working
    // state. This matters here specifically because firmware left this
    // controller not just running but with a REAL device already fully
    // enumerated on port 3 (Step 2's own finding: PORTSC3 showed
    // CCS=1/PED=1/PLS=0 U0, i.e. firmware's own xHCI driver already ran
    // Enable Slot/Address Device for that port, meaning firmware's own
    // DCBAA/device-context/event-ring state was ACTIVELY in use, not
    // idle). A first attempt at Step 6 without HCRST wrote a fresh,
    // otherwise-correct DCBAAP/CRCR/ERSTBA/ERDP sequence (byte-order
    // matching Linux's own xhci-mem.c exactly) and rang the Enable Slot
    // doorbell, but no Command Completion Event ever appeared -- HCRST
    // is the architected way to force the controller to fully discard
    // whatever internal state it already had (including firmware's own)
    // and adopt a genuinely fresh configuration, which a soft RS-cycle
    // does not guarantee.
    let mut hcrst_confirmed: bool = false;
    if (halt_confirmed) {
        usb_smoke_checkpoint = 45;
        let usbcmd_hcrst_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200020 as *io u32;
        let usbcmd_before_hcrst: u32 = *usbcmd_hcrst_ptr;
        *usbcmd_hcrst_ptr = usbcmd_before_hcrst | 2;

        let mut hcrst_polls: usize = 0;
        for i: usize in 0..<1000 {
            usb_smoke_checkpoint = 46;
            let usbcmd_poll_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200020 as *io u32;
            let usbcmd_poll: u32 = *usbcmd_poll_ptr;
            let usbsts_poll2_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200024 as *io u32;
            let usbsts_poll2: u32 = *usbsts_poll2_ptr;
            hcrst_polls = i + 1;
            // HCRST self-clears (bit1) AND CNR (USBSTS bit11) must also
            // clear before the controller is genuinely ready again.
            if (((usbcmd_poll >> 1) & 1) == 0 && ((usbsts_poll2 >> 11) & 1) == 0) {
                hcrst_confirmed = true;
                break;
            }
            delay_us(1000);
        }
        uart_puts("HCRST confirmed: ");
        if (hcrst_confirmed) {
            uart_puts("yes, polls=");
            uart_print(hcrst_polls);
        } else {
            uart_puts("NO (timed out)");
        }
        uart_puts("\r\n");

        // Isolation test (2026-07-26): check USBSTS.HCE (Host Controller
        // Error, bit12) RIGHT HERE, before touching DCBAAP/CRCR/CONFIG/
        // Enable Slot at all -- if HCE is already set immediately after
        // HCRST itself, the later fault has nothing to do with this
        // example's own Command/Event Ring setup at all.
        if (hcrst_confirmed) {
            let usbsts_post_hcrst_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200024 as *io u32;
            let usbsts_post_hcrst: u32 = *usbsts_post_hcrst_ptr;
            uart_puts("USBSTS immediately after HCRST: ");
            uart_print_hex(usbsts_post_hcrst as i32);
            uart_puts(" HCE=");
            uart_print(((usbsts_post_hcrst >> 12) & 1) as usize);
            uart_puts(" HSE=");
            uart_print(((usbsts_post_hcrst >> 2) & 1) as usize);
            uart_puts("\r\n");
        }
    }

    // Step 6 (2026-07-26 follow-up): only proceeds if HCRST was
    // confirmed -- DCBAAP/CRCR/CONFIG are only legal to write while
    // halted, so a failed reset makes everything below meaningless.
    if (hcrst_confirmed) {
        // Real DMA needs RP1-as-bus-master to actually be able to reach
        // CPU/system RAM over PCIe -- examples/common_rpi5/pcie.tkb's
        // own pcie2_dma_inbound_setup (added alongside this step) sets
        // up BCM2712's PCIe2 root complex RC_BAR2 inbound window for
        // exactly this. A first attempt at this whole step, WITHOUT
        // this call, found EHB (Event Ring's own "an event was posted"
        // bit) getting set by hardware but every dumped Event Ring TRB
        // reading back as zero -- the controller believed it wrote an
        // event, but that write never reached anywhere the CPU could
        // see, exactly the "inbound DMA window not configured" failure
        // mode this call fixes.
        //
        // ROOT CAUSE FOUND (2026-07-26), after four isolation tests each
        // ruled out a different hypothesis: the window itself was set up
        // correctly, but it was pointed at the WRONG PCI address. RP1
        // bus masters do NOT reach system RAM at PCI address ==
        // cpu_phys; Raspberry Pi 5's own device tree (bcm2712.dtsi,
        // pcie2's `dma-ranges`) says PCI [0, 4MB) maps to CPU
        // 0x1F_00000000 (RP1's OWN peripheral aperture!) and system RAM
        // is reachable only at PCI 0x10_00000000 + cpu_phys. Handing the
        // XHCI controller raw CPU physical addresses therefore pointed
        // it at RP1's own registers instead of at the Command Ring --
        // hence USBSTS.HSE (Host System Error) for ANY command TRB.
        // Every DMA address below now adds RP1_DMA_PCI_OFFSET
        // (examples/common_rpi5/pcie.tkb), and the window itself maps
        // PCI 0x10_00000000 -> CPU 0, matching the device tree exactly.
        pcie2_dma_inbound_setup();
        let rc_bar2_lo_diag_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1000124034 as *io u32;
        let rc_bar2_hi_diag_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1000124038 as *io u32;
        let ubus_bar2_lo_diag_ptr: *io u32 = 0x10001240B4 as *io u32;
        let ubus_bar2_hi_diag_ptr: *io u32 = 0x10001240B8 as *io u32;
        uart_puts("RC_BAR2_LO: ");
        uart_print_hex((*rc_bar2_lo_diag_ptr) as i32);
        uart_puts(" RC_BAR2_HI: ");
        uart_print_hex((*rc_bar2_hi_diag_ptr) as i32);
        uart_puts("\r\nUBUS_BAR2_LO: ");
        uart_print_hex((*ubus_bar2_lo_diag_ptr) as i32);
        uart_puts(" UBUS_BAR2_HI: ");
        uart_print_hex((*ubus_bar2_hi_diag_ptr) as i32);
        uart_puts("\r\n");

        // Scratchpad Buffer Array: HCSPARAMS2 (usb_smoke_host0_hcsparams2
        // = 0x140000f1) reports Max Scratchpad Buffers = 2 (Hi bits[25:21]
        // = 0, Lo bits[31:27] = 2) -- DCBAA[0] MUST point to a real
        // Scratchpad Buffer Array when this is nonzero, or the controller
        // may fault/misbehave whenever it actually needs scratchpad
        // space (spec requirement, not optional for this board).
        // Every *_dma value below is the CPU physical address PLUS
        // RP1_DMA_PCI_OFFSET -- that sum is what the controller must be
        // told, since it is a PCI address, not a CPU address (see the
        // root-cause note above). The plain *_base values stay CPU
        // addresses, since those are what THIS code dereferences and
        // what the cache-maintenance helpers take.
        usb_smoke_checkpoint = 50;
        let scratchpad_array_base: usize = (usb_smoke_scratchpad_array as *u8) as usize;
        let scratchpad_buf0_base: usize = (usb_smoke_scratchpad_buf0 as *u8) as usize;
        let scratchpad_buf1_base: usize = (usb_smoke_scratchpad_buf1 as *u8) as usize;
        let scratchpad_array_dma: usize = scratchpad_array_base + RP1_DMA_PCI_OFFSET;
        let scratchpad_buf0_dma: usize = scratchpad_buf0_base + RP1_DMA_PCI_OFFSET;
        let scratchpad_buf1_dma: usize = scratchpad_buf1_base + RP1_DMA_PCI_OFFSET;
        let sp0_ptr: *u32 = scratchpad_array_base as *u32;
        *sp0_ptr = (scratchpad_buf0_dma & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
        let sp0_hi_ptr: *u32 = (scratchpad_array_base + 4) as *u32;
        *sp0_hi_ptr = ((scratchpad_buf0_dma >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
        let sp1_ptr: *u32 = (scratchpad_array_base + 8) as *u32;
        *sp1_ptr = (scratchpad_buf1_dma & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
        let sp1_hi_ptr: *u32 = (scratchpad_array_base + 12) as *u32;
        *sp1_hi_ptr = ((scratchpad_buf1_dma >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
        rpi5_dcache_clean_range(scratchpad_array_base, 16);

        // DCBAA[0] = Scratchpad Buffer Array pointer. Isolation test
        // (2026-07-26) confirmed this is NOT the cause of the
        // USBSTS.HSE/HCE fault seen after ringing the Enable Slot
        // doorbell: leaving DCBAA[0]=0 (BSS default, no scratchpad
        // pointer at all -- a real spec violation given HCSPARAMS2's
        // Max Scratchpad Buffers=2, but diagnostically informative)
        // produced the IDENTICAL fault. Restored since it is still
        // spec-required and structurally correct regardless.
        usb_smoke_checkpoint = 51;
        let dcbaa_base: usize = (usb_smoke_dcbaa as *u8) as usize;
        let dcbaa_dma: usize = dcbaa_base + RP1_DMA_PCI_OFFSET;
        let dcbaa0_ptr: *u32 = dcbaa_base as *u32;
        *dcbaa0_ptr = (scratchpad_array_dma & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
        let dcbaa0_hi_ptr: *u32 = (dcbaa_base + 4) as *u32;
        *dcbaa0_hi_ptr = ((scratchpad_array_dma >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
        rpi5_dcache_clean_range(dcbaa_base, 8);

        // Command Ring: TRB[0] = Enable Slot Command (Parameter=0,
        // Status=0, Cycle=1 | TRB Type=9<<10 = 0x2401). TRB[3] = Link TRB
        // pointing back to TRB[0] (Cycle=1 | Toggle Cycle=1<<1 |
        // TRB Type=6<<10 = 0x1803).
        usb_smoke_checkpoint = 52;
        let cmd_ring_base: usize = (usb_smoke_cmd_ring as *u8) as usize;
        let cmd_ring_dma: usize = cmd_ring_base + RP1_DMA_PCI_OFFSET;
        let trb0_dw0_ptr: *u32 = cmd_ring_base as *u32;
        *trb0_dw0_ptr = 0;
        let trb0_dw1_ptr: *u32 = (cmd_ring_base + 4) as *u32;
        *trb0_dw1_ptr = 0;
        let trb0_dw2_ptr: *u32 = (cmd_ring_base + 8) as *u32;
        *trb0_dw2_ptr = 0;
        let trb0_dw3_ptr: *u32 = (cmd_ring_base + 12) as *u32;
        // Isolation test (2026-07-26) confirmed the fault is NOT
        // specific to Enable Slot: temporarily swapping this to a
        // No-Op Command TRB (Type 23, Cycle=1 | 23<<10 = 0x00005C01)
        // produced the IDENTICAL USBSTS.HSE/HCE fault. Restored to
        // Enable Slot since that is what this milestone actually needs;
        // the fault is in the Command Ring/doorbell mechanism itself,
        // not any one command's own semantics.
        *trb0_dw3_ptr = 0x00002401;

        // Link TRB's own pointer is a DMA address the CONTROLLER
        // follows, so it needs the PCI offset too -- not the raw CPU
        // address this code would use to dereference the same ring.
        let cmd_link_off: usize = (CMD_RING_TRBS - 1) * 16;
        let link_dw0_ptr: *u32 = (cmd_ring_base + cmd_link_off) as *u32;
        *link_dw0_ptr = (cmd_ring_dma & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
        let link_dw1_ptr: *u32 = (cmd_ring_base + cmd_link_off + 4) as *u32;
        *link_dw1_ptr = ((cmd_ring_dma >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
        let link_dw2_ptr: *u32 = (cmd_ring_base + cmd_link_off + 8) as *u32;
        *link_dw2_ptr = 0;
        let link_dw3_ptr: *u32 = (cmd_ring_base + cmd_link_off + 12) as *u32;
        *link_dw3_ptr = 0x00001803;
        usb_smoke_checkpoint = 53;
        rpi5_dcache_clean_range(cmd_ring_base, CMD_RING_TRBS * 16);

        // Event Ring Segment Table: one entry, base = event ring, size =
        // 16 TRBs.
        usb_smoke_checkpoint = 54;
        let evt_ring_base: usize = (usb_smoke_evt_ring as *u8) as usize;
        let erst_base: usize = (usb_smoke_erst as *u8) as usize;
        let evt_ring_dma: usize = evt_ring_base + RP1_DMA_PCI_OFFSET;
        let erst_dma: usize = erst_base + RP1_DMA_PCI_OFFSET;
        let erst_dw0_ptr: *u32 = erst_base as *u32;
        *erst_dw0_ptr = (evt_ring_dma & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
        let erst_dw1_ptr: *u32 = (erst_base + 4) as *u32;
        *erst_dw1_ptr = ((evt_ring_dma >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
        let erst_dw2_ptr: *u32 = (erst_base + 8) as *u32;
        *erst_dw2_ptr = 16; // Ring Segment Size (bits[15:0]) = 16 TRBs
        usb_smoke_checkpoint = 55;
        rpi5_dcache_clean_range(erst_base, 16);

        // Runtime registers: Interrupter 0 is at Capability base + RTSOFF
        // + 0x20 (RTSOFF itself, read in Step 4 as 0x460, points to
        // MFINDEX; interrupters start 0x20 further in). Programming
        // order: ERSTSZ, then ERSTBA, then ERDP LAST -- matching Linux's
        // own xhci_add_interrupter() exactly (drivers/usb/host/
        // xhci-mem.c), fetched and cross-checked after this step's FIRST
        // attempt (ERSTSZ/ERDP/ERSTBA-last, ERDP before ERSTBA) silently
        // never produced a Command Completion Event: CRCR.CRR read back
        // as 1 (Command Ring "running") but every Event Ring TRB stayed
        // zero, and EHB going from 0 -> 1 -> back to 0 across the run
        // turned out to be misleading (Linux's own xhci-mem.c comment:
        // "Don't clear the EHB bit... because there might be more events
        // to service" -- it is NOT meant to be explicitly cleared by
        // software at init, contrary to an earlier version of this file
        // that did so). Writing ERDP before ERSTBA plausibly let the
        // controller latch an undefined/stale ERSTBA-relative dequeue
        // interpretation that a later ERSTBA write never re-triggered.
        usb_smoke_checkpoint = 56;
        let ir0_base: usize = 0x1F00200000 + 0x460 + 0x20;
        let erstsz_ptr: *io u32 = ir0_base as *io u32;
        *erstsz_ptr = 1;
        usb_smoke_checkpoint = 57;
        let erstba_lo_ptr: *io u32 = (ir0_base + 0x10) as *io u32;
        *erstba_lo_ptr = (erst_dma & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
        let erstba_hi_ptr: *io u32 = (ir0_base + 0x14) as *io u32;
        *erstba_hi_ptr = ((erst_dma >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
        usb_smoke_checkpoint = 58;
        let erdp_lo_ptr: *io u32 = (ir0_base + 0x18) as *io u32;
        *erdp_lo_ptr = (evt_ring_dma & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
        let erdp_hi_ptr: *io u32 = (ir0_base + 0x1C) as *io u32;
        *erdp_hi_ptr = ((evt_ring_dma >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;

        // DCBAAP (Operational+0x30).
        usb_smoke_checkpoint = 59;
        let dcbaap_lo_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200050 as *io u32;
        *dcbaap_lo_ptr = (dcbaa_dma & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
        let dcbaap_hi_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200054 as *io u32;
        *dcbaap_hi_ptr = ((dcbaa_dma >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;

        // CRCR (Operational+0x18): pointer | RCS=1 (bit0). CS/CA (bits
        // 2:1) left clear -- this is ring INITIALIZATION, not a stop/
        // abort request.
        usb_smoke_checkpoint = 60;
        let crcr_lo_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200038 as *io u32;
        *crcr_lo_ptr = ((cmd_ring_dma & 0xFFFFFFC0) | 1) as u32;
        let crcr_hi_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F0020003C as *io u32;
        *crcr_hi_ptr = ((cmd_ring_dma >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;

        // CONFIG (Operational+0x38): MaxSlotsEn=1 -- this example only
        // ever enables one slot for the one flash drive on port 3.
        usb_smoke_checkpoint = 61;
        let config_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200058 as *io u32;
        *config_ptr = 1;

        // Restart the controller (USBCMD.RS=1). Reads USBCMD fresh here
        // (not the pre-HCRST usbcmd_after snapshot) -- HCRST resets
        // USBCMD to its own architectural default, so preserving
        // whatever bits happen to be there NOW is correct; the earlier
        // (pre-reset) INTE/HSEE snapshot is stale after a real reset.
        usb_smoke_checkpoint = 62;
        let usbcmd_restart_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200020 as *io u32;
        let usbcmd_fresh: u32 = *usbcmd_restart_ptr;
        let usbcmd_restart_val: u32 = usbcmd_fresh | 1;
        *usbcmd_restart_ptr = usbcmd_restart_val;

        let mut run_confirmed: bool = false;
        let mut run_polls: usize = 0;
        for i: usize in 0..<100 {
            usb_smoke_checkpoint = 63;
            let usbsts_run_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200024 as *io u32;
            let usbsts_run: u32 = *usbsts_run_ptr;
            run_polls = i + 1;
            if ((usbsts_run & 1) == 0) {
                run_confirmed = true;
                break;
            }
            delay_us(1000);
        }
        uart_puts("controller restarted: ");
        if (run_confirmed) {
            uart_puts("yes, polls=");
            uart_print(run_polls);
        } else {
            uart_puts("NO (timed out)");
        }
        uart_puts("\r\n");

        // Isolation test (2026-07-26): check USBSTS.HCE/HSE HERE, after
        // DCBAAP/CRCR/CONFIG/RS=1-restart but BEFORE ringing the Command
        // doorbell at all -- if the fault is already present at this
        // point, it comes from the register setup itself (Scratchpad/
        // DCBAAP/CRCR/CONFIG/restart), not from the doorbell ring or any
        // actual TRB fetch attempt.
        if (run_confirmed) {
            let usbsts_pre_doorbell_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200024 as *io u32;
            let usbsts_pre_doorbell: u32 = *usbsts_pre_doorbell_ptr;
            uart_puts("USBSTS before ringing doorbell: ");
            uart_print_hex(usbsts_pre_doorbell as i32);
            uart_puts(" HCE=");
            uart_print(((usbsts_pre_doorbell >> 12) & 1) as usize);
            uart_puts(" HSE=");
            uart_print(((usbsts_pre_doorbell >> 2) & 1) as usize);
            uart_puts("\r\n");
        }

        if (run_confirmed) {
            // Ring the Command doorbell: Doorbell Array is at Capability
            // base + DBOFF (Step 4: 0x500). Doorbell register 0 (the
            // Host Controller's own doorbell, used for the Command Ring)
            // -- writing 0 to its Target field enqueues/executes pending
            // Command Ring TRBs.
            usb_smoke_checkpoint = 64;
            let doorbell0_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200500 as *io u32;
            *doorbell0_ptr = 0;

            // Wait for the Command Completion Event (TRB Type 33). The
            // shared consumer keeps ERDP and the Cycle State straight
            // and invalidates the cache line before every check -- this
            // is real DMA-written memory, and a stale cached read would
            // look identical to "no event yet".
            usb_smoke_checkpoint = 65;
            let enable_cc: u32 = usb_await_event(33, 1000);
            let evt_confirmed: bool = enable_cc != 0;
            let evt_dw3: u32 = usb_xhci_last_evt_dw3;

            uart_puts("enable slot completion event: ");
            if (evt_confirmed) {
                uart_puts("yes, completion_code=");
                uart_print_hex(enable_cc as i32);
                uart_puts(" slot_id=");
                uart_print_hex(((evt_dw3 >> 24) & 0xFF) as i32);
                uart_puts(" trb_type=");
                uart_print_hex(((evt_dw3 >> 10) & 0x3F) as i32);
                uart_puts("\r\n");

                // Step 7 (2026-07-26 follow-up): Address Device, now that
                // Enable Slot has returned a real slot_id. Builds an
                // Input Context (Input Control Context + Slot Context +
                // Endpoint 0 Context, each 64-byte strided -- HCCPARAMS1
                // CSZ=1, confirmed in Step 1's own read), points
                // DCBAA[slot_id] at a fresh (zeroed) Device Context for
                // the controller to write its own OUTPUT Slot/EP0
                // Context into, and allocates Endpoint 0's own Transfer
                // Ring. All DMA-target addresses get RP1_DMA_PCI_OFFSET,
                // matching the Step 6 fix.
                let slot_id: usize = ((evt_dw3 >> 24) & 0xFF) as usize;
                usb_xhci_slot_id = slot_id;

                usb_smoke_checkpoint = 70;
                let dcbaa_slot_ptr: *u32 = (dcbaa_base + slot_id * 8) as *u32;
                let device_ctx_base: usize = (usb_smoke_device_ctx as *u8) as usize;
                let device_ctx_dma: usize = device_ctx_base + RP1_DMA_PCI_OFFSET;
                *dcbaa_slot_ptr = (device_ctx_dma & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
                let dcbaa_slot_hi_ptr: *u32 = (dcbaa_base + slot_id * 8 + 4) as *u32;
                *dcbaa_slot_hi_ptr = ((device_ctx_dma >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
                rpi5_dcache_clean_range(dcbaa_base + slot_id * 8, 8);

                // Endpoint 0 Transfer Ring: EP0_RING_TRBS entries, the
                // last of which is the Link TRB pointing back to the
                // start with Toggle Cycle set. TRB[0..N-2] stay zeroed
                // (Cycle=0, "not yet owned by hardware") until a real
                // control transfer writes into them; the Link TRB gets
                // Cycle=1, matching the ring's initial Producer/Consumer
                // Cycle State of 1.
                usb_smoke_checkpoint = 71;
                let ep0_ring_base: usize = (usb_smoke_ep0_ring as *u8) as usize;
                let ep0_ring_dma: usize = ep0_ring_base + RP1_DMA_PCI_OFFSET;
                let ep0_link_off: usize = (EP0_RING_TRBS - 1) * 16;
                let ep0_link_dw0_ptr: *u32 = (ep0_ring_base + ep0_link_off) as *u32;
                *ep0_link_dw0_ptr = (ep0_ring_dma & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
                let ep0_link_dw1_ptr: *u32 = (ep0_ring_base + ep0_link_off + 4) as *u32;
                *ep0_link_dw1_ptr = ((ep0_ring_dma >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
                let ep0_link_dw2_ptr: *u32 = (ep0_ring_base + ep0_link_off + 8) as *u32;
                *ep0_link_dw2_ptr = 0;
                let ep0_link_dw3_ptr: *u32 = (ep0_ring_base + ep0_link_off + 12) as *u32;
                *ep0_link_dw3_ptr = 0x00001803; // Cycle=1 | TC=1<<1 | Link(6)<<10
                rpi5_dcache_clean_range(ep0_ring_base, EP0_RING_TRBS * 16);
                usb_xhci_ep0_enq = 0;
                usb_xhci_ep0_cycle = 1;

                // Input Context: Input Control Context (offset 0).
                // add_flags bit0 (A0, Slot) | bit1 (A1, EP0) = 3.
                usb_smoke_checkpoint = 72;
                let input_ctx_base: usize = (usb_smoke_input_ctx as *u8) as usize;
                let input_ctx_dma: usize = input_ctx_base + RP1_DMA_PCI_OFFSET;
                let drop_flags_ptr: *u32 = input_ctx_base as *u32;
                *drop_flags_ptr = 0;
                let add_flags_ptr: *u32 = (input_ctx_base + 4) as *u32;
                *add_flags_ptr = 3;

                // Slot Context (offset 64): Route String=0 (directly on
                // the root port, no hub in between), Speed = the SAME
                // value PORTSC3 already reported (bits[13:10]=4,
                // SuperSpeed -- read directly off real hardware in Step
                // 2, not assumed from a generic "USB3 = SS" mapping),
                // Context Entries=1 (only EP0 configured so far), Root
                // Hub Port Number=3 (usbhost0 port 3, where the flash
                // drive is).
                usb_smoke_checkpoint = 73;
                let slot_speed: u32 = (usb_smoke_host0_portsc3 >> 10) & 0xF;
                let slot_ctx_base: usize = input_ctx_base + 64;
                let slot_dev_info_ptr: *u32 = slot_ctx_base as *u32;
                *slot_dev_info_ptr = (slot_speed << 20) | (1 << 27);
                let slot_dev_info2_ptr: *u32 = (slot_ctx_base + 4) as *u32;
                *slot_dev_info2_ptr = 3 << 16; // ROOT_HUB_PORT(3)
                let slot_tt_info_ptr: *u32 = (slot_ctx_base + 8) as *u32;
                *slot_tt_info_ptr = 0;
                let slot_dev_state_ptr: *u32 = (slot_ctx_base + 12) as *u32;
                *slot_dev_state_ptr = 0;

                // Endpoint 0 Context (offset 128): EP Type=4 (Control
                // Bidirectional) | Error Count=3 | Max Packet Size=512
                // (SuperSpeed control endpoint, USB 3.0 spec's own fixed
                // value -- unlike HS/FS/LS, SuperSpeed does not negotiate
                // this via the device descriptor's bMaxPacketSize0).
                // Average TRB Length=8 (a Setup Stage TRB's own fixed
                // size). TR Dequeue Pointer = EP0 ring's DMA address |
                // Cycle State (1, matching the ring's initial PCS/DCS).
                usb_smoke_checkpoint = 74;
                let ep0_ctx_base: usize = input_ctx_base + 128;
                let ep0_ep_info_ptr: *u32 = ep0_ctx_base as *u32;
                *ep0_ep_info_ptr = 0;
                let ep0_ep_info2_ptr: *u32 = (ep0_ctx_base + 4) as *u32;
                *ep0_ep_info2_ptr = (4 << 3) | (3 << 1) | (512 << 16);
                let ep0_deq_lo_ptr: *u32 = (ep0_ctx_base + 8) as *u32;
                *ep0_deq_lo_ptr = ((ep0_ring_dma & 0xFFFFFFFF) | 1) as u32;
                let ep0_deq_hi_ptr: *u32 = (ep0_ctx_base + 12) as *u32;
                *ep0_deq_hi_ptr = ((ep0_ring_dma >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
                let ep0_tx_info_ptr: *u32 = (ep0_ctx_base + 16) as *u32;
                *ep0_tx_info_ptr = 8; // EP_AVG_TRB_LENGTH(8)
                rpi5_dcache_clean_range(input_ctx_base, INPUT_CTX_SIZE);

                // USB Port Reset (PORTSC.PR, bit4) -- REQUIRED before
                // Address Device: the first real-hardware attempt at
                // this step (without a reset) got a genuine Command
                // Completion Event back (proving the DMA/command
                // machinery all now works, per the Step 6 fix above) but
                // with completion_code=4 (USB Transaction Error) --
                // Address Device implicitly performs a real SET_ADDRESS
                // transaction on the wire, and this device was already
                // enumerated by FIRMWARE before this example ever ran
                // (Step 2's own PORTSC3 finding: PLS=U0 at handoff).
                // USBCMD.HCRST resets the CONTROLLER's own internal
                // state but does NOT reset the downstream USB DEVICE
                // itself -- only a real Port Reset does that, putting
                // the device back in the USB "Default" state a fresh
                // SET_ADDRESS actually expects.
                //
                // 0xCE00FFE9 is Linux's own xhci_port_state_to_neutral()
                // mask, reproduced exactly (drivers/usb/host/xhci.h):
                //   XHCI_PORT_RO  = (1<<0)|(1<<3)|(0xf<<10)|(1<<30)|(1<<31)
                //   XHCI_PORT_RWS = (0xf<<5)|(1<<9)|(0x3<<14)|(0x7<<25)
                //   neutral = state & (XHCI_PORT_RO | XHCI_PORT_RWS)
                // i.e. keep ONLY the read-only and read-write-shared
                // fields, and write ZERO to everything else.
                //
                // Getting this mask wrong is not cosmetic. PORTSC bit1
                // (PED) is RW1CS: reading 1 means "enabled", but WRITING
                // 1 DISABLES the port. An earlier version of this code
                // used 0xFF01FFFF (masking only the change bits 23:17),
                // which preserved the read-back PED=1 and therefore
                // disabled the port on every read-modify-write -- once
                // here and again in the PRC clear below, the latter
                // landing immediately before Address Device. The result
                // on real hardware was Address Device failing with
                // completion_code=0x04 (USB Transaction Error) while the
                // same command with BSR=1 (context-only, no bus traffic)
                // succeeded -- the device was simply unreachable because
                // we had just switched its port off. Bit4 (PR) is
                // likewise write-only-meaningful and must come from the
                // OR below, never from the read-back value.
                usb_smoke_checkpoint = 79;
                let portsc3_reset_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200440 as *io u32;
                let portsc3_before_reset: u32 = *portsc3_reset_ptr;
                *portsc3_reset_ptr = (portsc3_before_reset & 0xCE00FFE9) | (1 << 4);

                let mut reset_confirmed: bool = false;
                let mut reset_polls: usize = 0;
                for i: usize in 0..<500 {
                    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 80;
                    let portsc3_poll_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200440 as *io u32;
                    let portsc3_poll: u32 = *portsc3_poll_ptr;
                    reset_polls = i + 1;
                    if (((portsc3_poll >> 21) & 1) == 1) {
                        reset_confirmed = true;
                        break;
                    }
                    delay_us(1000);
                }

                let portsc3_after_reset_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200440 as *io u32;
                let portsc3_after_reset: u32 = *portsc3_after_reset_ptr;
                uart_puts("port reset (PRC) confirmed: ");
                if (reset_confirmed) {
                    uart_puts("yes, polls=");
                    uart_print(reset_polls);
                } else {
                    uart_puts("NO (timed out)");
                }
                uart_puts(" PORTSC3=");
                uart_print_hex(portsc3_after_reset as i32);
                uart_puts("\r\n");

                // Clear the change bits the reset raised (RW1C: write 1
                // to clear), over the same neutral mask as above. PRC
                // plus CSC (bit17) and WRC (bit19), which a SuperSpeed
                // port reset also sets -- leaving them latched would
                // make every later PORTSC read look like a fresh event.
                if (reset_confirmed) {
                    let portsc3_clear_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200440 as *io u32;
                    let portsc3_before_clear: u32 = *portsc3_clear_ptr;
                    *portsc3_clear_ptr = (portsc3_before_clear & 0xCE00FFE9)
                        | (1 << 21) | (1 << 17) | (1 << 19);
                }

                // USB reset recovery time (TRSTRCY, USB2 spec 7.1.7.3,
                // also observed by USB3 hubs): the device needs >=10ms
                // after reset before it will reliably answer SET_ADDRESS.
                // Linux's own hub_port_reset() waits 10ms + 40ms margin
                // (see drivers/usb/core/hub.c reset_recovery_time) --
                // matched here; without it Address Device on real
                // hardware fails with completion_code=0x04 (USB
                // Transaction Error).
                delay_us(50000);

                // Re-read PORTSC3 once the recovery wait is over: PED
                // (bit1) must still read 1 and PLS (bits 8:5) must be 0
                // (U0) here, or the port is not actually usable and
                // Address Device cannot possibly reach the device.
                let portsc3_ready_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200440 as *io u32;
                let portsc3_ready: u32 = *portsc3_ready_ptr;
                uart_puts("PORTSC3 before address device: ");
                uart_print_hex(portsc3_ready as i32);
                uart_puts("\r\n");

                // Address Device Command TRB, into Command Ring TRB[1]
                // (TRB[0] already holds the consumed Enable Slot command
                // -- this is the ring's own second entry, still within
                // its un-wrapped Producer Cycle State of 1). Parameter =
                // Input Context DMA address. DWORD3: Cycle=1 | BSR=0
                // (bit9, Block Set Address Request -- 0 means actually
                // issue SET_ADDRESS to the device, not just validate the
                // context) | TRB Type=11 (Address Device)<<10 |
                // Slot ID<<24.
                usb_smoke_checkpoint = 75;
                let trb1_dw0_ptr: *u32 = (cmd_ring_base + 16) as *u32;
                *trb1_dw0_ptr = (input_ctx_dma & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
                let trb1_dw1_ptr: *u32 = (cmd_ring_base + 20) as *u32;
                *trb1_dw1_ptr = ((input_ctx_dma >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
                let trb1_dw2_ptr: *u32 = (cmd_ring_base + 24) as *u32;
                *trb1_dw2_ptr = 0;
                let trb1_dw3_ptr: *u32 = (cmd_ring_base + 28) as *u32;
                *trb1_dw3_ptr = (1 | (11 << 10) | (slot_id << 24)) as u32;
                rpi5_dcache_clean_range(cmd_ring_base + 16, 16);

                // Ring the Command doorbell again -- the controller's own
                // internal Command Ring dequeue pointer is already past
                // TRB[0] (it just completed that command), so this makes
                // it fetch and execute TRB[1] (Address Device).
                usb_smoke_checkpoint = 77;
                *doorbell0_ptr = 0;

                // The Port Reset above queues its OWN async Port Status
                // Change Event (TRB Type 34) into the ring, which lands
                // BEFORE Address Device's real Command Completion Event
                // (TRB Type 33) -- confirmed on real hardware: an
                // earlier fixed-index read picked up the Port Status
                // Change Event instead. usb_await_event() scans past
                // anything that is not the wanted type.
                usb_smoke_checkpoint = 78;
                let addr_cc: u32 = usb_await_event(33, 1000);
                let addr_confirmed: bool = addr_cc != 0;
                let addr_dw3: u32 = usb_xhci_last_evt_dw3;

                uart_puts("address device completion event: ");
                if (addr_confirmed) {
                    uart_puts("yes, completion_code=");
                    uart_print_hex(addr_cc as i32);
                    uart_puts(" slot_id=");
                    uart_print_hex(((addr_dw3 >> 24) & 0xFF) as i32);
                    uart_puts(" trb_type=");
                    uart_print_hex(((addr_dw3 >> 10) & 0x3F) as i32);
                } else {
                    uart_puts("NO (timed out)");
                    let usbsts_addr_diag_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200024 as *io u32;
                    let usbsts_addr_diag: u32 = *usbsts_addr_diag_ptr;
                    uart_puts(" USBSTS=");
                    uart_print_hex(usbsts_addr_diag as i32);
                }
                uart_puts("\r\n");

                // Independent proof that Address Device really did its
                // bus-level work: the controller writes the OUTPUT
                // Device Context itself. Its Slot Context DWORD3 carries
                // USB Device Address (bits 7:0) -- nonzero only if a
                // real SET_ADDRESS was accepted by the device -- and
                // Slot State (bits 31:27), which must now read 2
                // (Addressed; 0=Disabled/Enabled, 1=Default,
                // 2=Addressed, 3=Configured).
                usb_smoke_checkpoint = 81;
                rpi5_dcache_invalidate_range(device_ctx_base, DEVICE_CTX_SIZE);
                let dev_slot_dw3_ptr: *u32 = (device_ctx_base + 12) as *u32;
                let dev_slot_dw3: u32 = *dev_slot_dw3_ptr;
                uart_puts("device context slot: usb_address=");
                uart_print_hex((dev_slot_dw3 & 0xFF) as i32);
                uart_puts(" slot_state=");
                uart_print_hex(((dev_slot_dw3 >> 27) & 0x1F) as i32);
                uart_puts("\r\n");

                // ------------------------------------------------------
                // Step 8: the first real control transfer --
                // GET_DESCRIPTOR(Device), 18 bytes, over EP0's own
                // Transfer Ring. Shape follows U-Boot's xhci_ctrl_tx():
                // three TRBs (Setup Stage, Data Stage, Status Stage)
                // queued back to back, then one doorbell ring, then one
                // Transfer Event.
                // ------------------------------------------------------
                if (addr_confirmed) {
                    let ctrl_buf_base: usize = (usb_smoke_ctrl_buf as *u8) as usize;
                    let ctrl_buf_dma: usize = ctrl_buf_base + RP1_DMA_PCI_OFFSET;

                    // Poison the landing buffer first so a printed
                    // descriptor can never be mistaken for leftover
                    // zeroes the controller never actually wrote.
                    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 82;
                    for i: usize in 0..<64 {
                        let poison_ptr: *u8 = (ctrl_buf_base + i) as *u8;
                        *poison_ptr = 0xEE;
                    }
                    rpi5_dcache_clean_range(ctrl_buf_base, 256);

                    // GET_DESCRIPTOR(Device): bmRequestType=0x80
                    // (device-to-host, standard, recipient device),
                    // bRequest=6, wValue=0x0100 (descriptor type 1 =
                    // Device, index 0), wLength=18.
                    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 83;
                    let dev_cc: u32 = usb_ctrl_xfer(0x80, 6, 0x0100, 0, 18,
                                                    ctrl_buf_dma, true);
                    uart_puts("control transfer (GET_DESCRIPTOR device): ");
                    if (dev_cc != 0) {
                        uart_puts("yes, completion_code=");
                        uart_print_hex(dev_cc as i32);
                        uart_puts(" residual=");
                        uart_print(usb_xhci_last_residual as usize);
                        uart_puts(" ep_id=");
                        uart_print_hex(((usb_xhci_last_evt_dw3 >> 16) & 0x1F) as i32);
                    } else {
                        uart_puts("NO (timed out)");
                        let usbsts_x_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200024 as *io u32;
                        let usbsts_x: u32 = *usbsts_x_ptr;
                        uart_puts(" USBSTS=");
                        uart_print_hex(usbsts_x as i32);
                    }
                    uart_puts("\r\n");

                    // Decode the Device Descriptor the controller DMAed
                    // in. Layout (USB 2.0 spec table 9-8): [0]=bLength
                    // (18), [1]=bDescriptorType (1), [4]=bDeviceClass,
                    // [7]=bMaxPacketSize0, [8..9]=idVendor,
                    // [10..11]=idProduct.
                    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 88;
                    rpi5_dcache_invalidate_range(ctrl_buf_base, 256);
                    let d_len_ptr: *u8 = ctrl_buf_base as *u8;
                    let d_type_ptr: *u8 = (ctrl_buf_base + 1) as *u8;
                    let d_class_ptr: *u8 = (ctrl_buf_base + 4) as *u8;
                    let d_mps0_ptr: *u8 = (ctrl_buf_base + 7) as *u8;
                    let d_vid_lo_ptr: *u8 = (ctrl_buf_base + 8) as *u8;
                    let d_vid_hi_ptr: *u8 = (ctrl_buf_base + 9) as *u8;
                    let d_pid_lo_ptr: *u8 = (ctrl_buf_base + 10) as *u8;
                    let d_pid_hi_ptr: *u8 = (ctrl_buf_base + 11) as *u8;
                    let vid: u32 = (*d_vid_lo_ptr as u32) | ((*d_vid_hi_ptr as u32) << 8);
                    let pid: u32 = (*d_pid_lo_ptr as u32) | ((*d_pid_hi_ptr as u32) << 8);
                    uart_puts("device descriptor: bLength=");
                    uart_print_hex(*d_len_ptr as i32);
                    uart_puts(" bDescriptorType=");
                    uart_print_hex(*d_type_ptr as i32);
                    uart_puts(" bDeviceClass=");
                    uart_print_hex(*d_class_ptr as i32);
                    uart_puts(" bMaxPacketSize0=");
                    uart_print_hex(*d_mps0_ptr as i32);
                    uart_puts("\r\n");
                    uart_puts("device descriptor: idVendor=");
                    uart_print_hex(vid as i32);
                    uart_puts(" idProduct=");
                    uart_print_hex(pid as i32);
                    uart_puts("\r\n");

                    // --------------------------------------------------
                    // Step 9: Configuration Descriptor. Read the 9-byte
                    // header first purely to learn wTotalLength (the
                    // configuration, interface, endpoint and companion
                    // descriptors are returned as one contiguous blob
                    // whose size is only known from that field), then
                    // re-read the whole thing.
                    // --------------------------------------------------
                    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 89;
                    let cfg9_cc: u32 = usb_ctrl_xfer(0x80, 6, 0x0200, 0, 9,
                                                     ctrl_buf_dma, true);
                    rpi5_dcache_invalidate_range(ctrl_buf_base, 256);
                    let tot_lo_ptr: *u8 = (ctrl_buf_base + 2) as *u8;
                    let tot_hi_ptr: *u8 = (ctrl_buf_base + 3) as *u8;
                    let mut total_len: usize =
                        ((*tot_lo_ptr as usize) | ((*tot_hi_ptr as usize) << 8));
                    uart_puts("config descriptor header: completion_code=");
                    uart_print_hex(cfg9_cc as i32);
                    uart_puts(" wTotalLength=");
                    uart_print(total_len);
                    uart_puts("\r\n");

                    // Clamp to the landing buffer. A Mass Storage
                    // configuration is well under 256 bytes, but a
                    // device is free to report anything here and this
                    // value drives a DMA length.
                    if (total_len > 256) {
                        total_len = 256;
                    }

                    if (cfg9_cc == 1 && total_len >= 9) {
                        usb_smoke_checkpoint = 90;
                        for i: usize in 0..<256 {
                            let poison_ptr: *u8 = (ctrl_buf_base + i) as *u8;
                            *poison_ptr = 0xEE;
                        }
                        rpi5_dcache_clean_range(ctrl_buf_base, 256);

                        let cfg_cc: u32 = usb_ctrl_xfer(0x80, 6, 0x0200, 0,
                                                        total_len as u32,
                                                        ctrl_buf_dma, true);
                        rpi5_dcache_invalidate_range(ctrl_buf_base, 256);
                        uart_puts("config descriptor full: completion_code=");
                        uart_print_hex(cfg_cc as i32);
                        uart_puts(" residual=");
                        uart_print(usb_xhci_last_residual as usize);
                        uart_puts("\r\n");

                        // Walk the blob. Every descriptor starts with
                        // bLength, bDescriptorType, so an unknown type
                        // is simply skipped by its own length -- which
                        // is the only reason a parser can survive
                        // vendor-specific descriptors it has never seen.
                        usb_smoke_checkpoint = 91;
                        let mut pos: usize = 0;
                        let mut in_msc_iface: bool = false;
                        let mut last_ep_in: bool = false;
                        let mut last_ep_bulk: bool = false;
                        for step: usize in 0..<64 {
                            if (pos + 2 > total_len) {
                                break;
                            }
                            let blen_ptr: *u8 = (ctrl_buf_base + pos) as *u8;
                            let btype_ptr: *u8 = (ctrl_buf_base + pos + 1) as *u8;
                            let blen: usize = *blen_ptr as usize;
                            let btype: u32 = *btype_ptr as u32;
                            if (blen < 2) {
                                break;
                            }

                            // Configuration Descriptor (type 2):
                            // bNumInterfaces[4], bConfigurationValue[5].
                            if (btype == 2 && blen >= 6) {
                                let nif_ptr: *u8 = (ctrl_buf_base + pos + 4) as *u8;
                                let cval_ptr: *u8 = (ctrl_buf_base + pos + 5) as *u8;
                                usb_msc_config_value = *cval_ptr as u32;
                                uart_puts("  configuration: bNumInterfaces=");
                                uart_print(*nif_ptr as usize);
                                uart_puts(" bConfigurationValue=");
                                uart_print(usb_msc_config_value as usize);
                                uart_puts("\r\n");
                            }

                            // Interface Descriptor (type 4). Mass
                            // Storage is class 8; subclass 6 is SCSI
                            // transparent command set and protocol 0x50
                            // is Bulk-Only Transport -- the exact
                            // combination usb_msc.tkb's BOT/SCSI layer
                            // on the RPi3 side already speaks.
                            if (btype == 4 && blen >= 9) {
                                let ifnum_ptr: *u8 = (ctrl_buf_base + pos + 2) as *u8;
                                let nep_ptr: *u8 = (ctrl_buf_base + pos + 4) as *u8;
                                let icls_ptr: *u8 = (ctrl_buf_base + pos + 5) as *u8;
                                let isub_ptr: *u8 = (ctrl_buf_base + pos + 6) as *u8;
                                let iproto_ptr: *u8 = (ctrl_buf_base + pos + 7) as *u8;
                                let icls: u32 = *icls_ptr as u32;
                                in_msc_iface = icls == 8;
                                if (in_msc_iface) {
                                    usb_msc_interface_num = *ifnum_ptr as u32;
                                }
                                uart_puts("  interface: num=");
                                uart_print(*ifnum_ptr as usize);
                                uart_puts(" bNumEndpoints=");
                                uart_print(*nep_ptr as usize);
                                uart_puts(" class=");
                                uart_print_hex(icls as i32);
                                uart_puts(" subclass=");
                                uart_print_hex(*isub_ptr as i32);
                                uart_puts(" protocol=");
                                uart_print_hex(*iproto_ptr as i32);
                                uart_puts("\r\n");
                            }

                            // Endpoint Descriptor (type 5).
                            // bEndpointAddress bit7 = direction (1=IN),
                            // bmAttributes bits1:0 = transfer type
                            // (2=Bulk).
                            if (btype == 5 && blen >= 7) {
                                let epa_ptr: *u8 = (ctrl_buf_base + pos + 2) as *u8;
                                let attr_ptr: *u8 = (ctrl_buf_base + pos + 3) as *u8;
                                let mps_lo_ptr: *u8 = (ctrl_buf_base + pos + 4) as *u8;
                                let mps_hi_ptr: *u8 = (ctrl_buf_base + pos + 5) as *u8;
                                let epa: u32 = *epa_ptr as u32;
                                let attr: u32 = *attr_ptr as u32;
                                let mps: u32 =
                                    (*mps_lo_ptr as u32) | ((*mps_hi_ptr as u32) << 8);
                                last_ep_in = (epa & 0x80) != 0;
                                last_ep_bulk = (attr & 3) == 2;
                                if (in_msc_iface && last_ep_bulk) {
                                    if (last_ep_in) {
                                        usb_msc_bulk_in_ep = epa;
                                        usb_msc_bulk_in_mps = mps;
                                    } else {
                                        usb_msc_bulk_out_ep = epa;
                                        usb_msc_bulk_out_mps = mps;
                                    }
                                }
                                uart_puts("  endpoint: addr=");
                                uart_print_hex(epa as i32);
                                uart_puts(" attr=");
                                uart_print_hex(attr as i32);
                                uart_puts(" wMaxPacketSize=");
                                uart_print(mps as usize);
                                uart_puts("\r\n");
                            }

                            // SuperSpeed Endpoint Companion (type 0x30)
                            // always immediately follows the endpoint
                            // it belongs to. bMaxBurst[2] is zero-based
                            // (0 means one packet per burst) and is
                            // required when configuring a SuperSpeed
                            // endpoint's context later.
                            if (btype == 0x30 && blen >= 6) {
                                let burst_ptr: *u8 = (ctrl_buf_base + pos + 2) as *u8;
                                let burst: u32 = *burst_ptr as u32;
                                if (in_msc_iface && last_ep_bulk) {
                                    if (last_ep_in) {
                                        usb_msc_bulk_in_burst = burst;
                                    } else {
                                        usb_msc_bulk_out_burst = burst;
                                    }
                                }
                                uart_puts("  ss companion: bMaxBurst=");
                                uart_print(burst as usize);
                                uart_puts("\r\n");
                            }

                            pos = pos + blen;
                        }

                        uart_puts("msc endpoints: bulk_in=");
                        uart_print_hex(usb_msc_bulk_in_ep as i32);
                        uart_puts(" (mps=");
                        uart_print(usb_msc_bulk_in_mps as usize);
                        uart_puts(" burst=");
                        uart_print(usb_msc_bulk_in_burst as usize);
                        uart_puts(") bulk_out=");
                        uart_print_hex(usb_msc_bulk_out_ep as i32);
                        uart_puts(" (mps=");
                        uart_print(usb_msc_bulk_out_mps as usize);
                        uart_puts(" burst=");
                        uart_print(usb_msc_bulk_out_burst as usize);
                        uart_puts(")\r\n");

                        // ----------------------------------------------
                        // Step 10: Configure Endpoint, then
                        // SET_CONFIGURATION. Order matters and is not
                        // obvious: both Linux (usb_hcd_alloc_bandwidth
                        // then the control request, in
                        // usb_set_configuration) and U-Boot
                        // (xhci_submit_control_msg intercepts
                        // SET_CONFIGURATION to run
                        // xhci_set_configuration FIRST) program the
                        // controller's endpoint contexts BEFORE telling
                        // the device to switch configuration.
                        // ----------------------------------------------
                        // Endpoint index, U-Boot's xhci_get_ep_index():
                        //   index = epnum*2 - (dir_in ? 0 : 1)
                        // DCI is index+1, and the Endpoint Context for
                        // DCI n sits at (n+1)*64 in the Input Context.
                        // For this device: EP 0x81 (IN, num 1) ->
                        // index 2, DCI 3; EP 0x02 (OUT, num 2) ->
                        // index 3, DCI 4.
                        //
                        // Both endpoint numbers must be nonzero: number
                        // 0 is the control endpoint and can never be a
                        // bulk endpoint, and the OUT index formula
                        // would underflow on it. The compiler rejects
                        // the subtraction without this guard, which is
                        // the right call -- a malformed descriptor
                        // really could report endpoint 0 here.
                        let in_num: usize = (usb_msc_bulk_in_ep & 0x0F) as usize;
                        let out_num: usize = (usb_msc_bulk_out_ep & 0x0F) as usize;
                        if (in_num > 0 && out_num > 0) {
                            let in_idx: usize = in_num * 2;
                            let out_idx: usize = out_num * 2 - 1;
                            let mut max_idx: usize = in_idx;
                            if (out_idx > max_idx) {
                                max_idx = out_idx;
                            }

                            usb_smoke_checkpoint = 92;
                            rpi5_dcache_invalidate_range(device_ctx_base, DEVICE_CTX_SIZE);

                            // Seed the Input Context from the
                            // controller's OUTPUT Device Context
                            // (U-Boot's xhci_slot_copy /
                            // xhci_endpoint_copy). The Slot Context
                            // already holds the USB address the
                            // controller assigned during Address
                            // Device; rebuilding it from scratch here
                            // would throw that away.
                            for i: usize in 0..<32 {
                                let src_ptr: *u8 = (device_ctx_base + i) as *u8;
                                let dst_ptr: *u8 = (input_ctx_base + CTX_STRIDE + i) as *u8;
                                *dst_ptr = *src_ptr;
                            }
                            for i: usize in 0..<32 {
                                let src_ptr: *u8 = (device_ctx_base + CTX_STRIDE + i) as *u8;
                                let dst_ptr: *u8 = (input_ctx_base + 2 * CTX_STRIDE + i) as *u8;
                                *dst_ptr = *src_ptr;
                            }

                            // Slot Context dev_info: replace Context
                            // Entries (bits 31:27) with the highest DCI
                            // now in use, leaving Route String and Speed
                            // exactly as the controller left them.
                            // 0x07FFFFFF = ~(0x1f << 27).
                            usb_smoke_checkpoint = 93;
                            let slot_dw0_ptr: *u32 = (input_ctx_base + CTX_STRIDE) as *u32;
                            let slot_dw0: u32 = *slot_dw0_ptr;
                            *slot_dw0_ptr =
                                (slot_dw0 & 0x07FFFFFF) | (((max_idx + 1) as u32) << 27);

                            // Bulk Endpoint Contexts. Field encodings
                            // are U-Boot's (include/usb/xhci.h):
                            //   ep_info  = EP_MAX_ESIT_PAYLOAD_HI |
                            //              EP_INTERVAL | EP_MULT -- all
                            //              zero for bulk.
                            //   ep_info2 = EP_TYPE(t)<<3 |
                            //              MAX_BURST(b)<<8 |
                            //              MAX_PACKET(m)<<16 |
                            //              ERROR_COUNT(3)<<1
                            //   deq      = ring DMA address | DCS
                            //   tx_info  = EP_AVG_TRB_LENGTH(avg)
                            // EP Type = (bmAttributes & 3) | (dir << 2),
                            // so Bulk OUT = 2 and Bulk IN = 6.
                            //
                            // Average TRB Length is left at 0 for bulk:
                            // it derives from Max ESIT Payload, which
                            // xHCI defines as 0 for control and bulk
                            // endpoints. Both Linux and U-Boot leave it
                            // zero here and only force 8 for control
                            // endpoints (xHCI 6.2.3).
                            usb_smoke_checkpoint = 94;
                            let bulk_in_ring_base: usize =
                                (usb_smoke_bulk_in_ring as *u8) as usize;
                            let bulk_in_ring_dma: usize =
                                bulk_in_ring_base + RP1_DMA_PCI_OFFSET;
                            let bulk_out_ring_base: usize =
                                (usb_smoke_bulk_out_ring as *u8) as usize;
                            let bulk_out_ring_dma: usize =
                                bulk_out_ring_base + RP1_DMA_PCI_OFFSET;
                            let bulk_link_off: usize = (BULK_RING_TRBS - 1) * 16;

                            let bin_link0_ptr: *u32 =
                                (bulk_in_ring_base + bulk_link_off) as *u32;
                            *bin_link0_ptr = (bulk_in_ring_dma & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
                            let bin_link1_ptr: *u32 =
                                (bulk_in_ring_base + bulk_link_off + 4) as *u32;
                            *bin_link1_ptr = ((bulk_in_ring_dma >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
                            let bin_link3_ptr: *u32 =
                                (bulk_in_ring_base + bulk_link_off + 12) as *u32;
                            *bin_link3_ptr = 0x00001803;
                            rpi5_dcache_clean_range(bulk_in_ring_base, BULK_RING_TRBS * 16);

                            let bout_link0_ptr: *u32 =
                                (bulk_out_ring_base + bulk_link_off) as *u32;
                            *bout_link0_ptr = (bulk_out_ring_dma & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
                            let bout_link1_ptr: *u32 =
                                (bulk_out_ring_base + bulk_link_off + 4) as *u32;
                            *bout_link1_ptr = ((bulk_out_ring_dma >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
                            let bout_link3_ptr: *u32 =
                                (bulk_out_ring_base + bulk_link_off + 12) as *u32;
                            *bout_link3_ptr = 0x00001803;
                            rpi5_dcache_clean_range(bulk_out_ring_base, BULK_RING_TRBS * 16);

                            let in_ctx_off: usize = (in_idx + 2) * CTX_STRIDE;
                            let in_ep_dw0_ptr: *u32 = (input_ctx_base + in_ctx_off) as *u32;
                            *in_ep_dw0_ptr = 0;
                            let in_ep_dw1_ptr: *u32 = (input_ctx_base + in_ctx_off + 4) as *u32;
                            *in_ep_dw1_ptr = (6 << 3) | (3 << 1)
                                | ((usb_msc_bulk_in_burst & 0xFF) << 8)
                                | ((usb_msc_bulk_in_mps & 0xFFFF) << 16);
                            let in_ep_dw2_ptr: *u32 = (input_ctx_base + in_ctx_off + 8) as *u32;
                            *in_ep_dw2_ptr = ((bulk_in_ring_dma & 0xFFFFFFFF) | 1) as u32;
                            let in_ep_dw3_ptr: *u32 = (input_ctx_base + in_ctx_off + 12) as *u32;
                            *in_ep_dw3_ptr = ((bulk_in_ring_dma >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
                            let in_ep_dw4_ptr: *u32 = (input_ctx_base + in_ctx_off + 16) as *u32;
                            *in_ep_dw4_ptr = 0;

                            let out_ctx_off: usize = (out_idx + 2) * CTX_STRIDE;
                            let out_ep_dw0_ptr: *u32 = (input_ctx_base + out_ctx_off) as *u32;
                            *out_ep_dw0_ptr = 0;
                            let out_ep_dw1_ptr: *u32 = (input_ctx_base + out_ctx_off + 4) as *u32;
                            *out_ep_dw1_ptr = (2 << 3) | (3 << 1)
                                | ((usb_msc_bulk_out_burst & 0xFF) << 8)
                                | ((usb_msc_bulk_out_mps & 0xFFFF) << 16);
                            let out_ep_dw2_ptr: *u32 = (input_ctx_base + out_ctx_off + 8) as *u32;
                            *out_ep_dw2_ptr = ((bulk_out_ring_dma & 0xFFFFFFFF) | 1) as u32;
                            let out_ep_dw3_ptr: *u32 = (input_ctx_base + out_ctx_off + 12) as *u32;
                            *out_ep_dw3_ptr = ((bulk_out_ring_dma >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
                            let out_ep_dw4_ptr: *u32 = (input_ctx_base + out_ctx_off + 16) as *u32;
                            *out_ep_dw4_ptr = 0;

                            // Input Control Context: add the Slot
                            // Context (A0) plus one bit per endpoint at
                            // (index + 1). EP0's A1 is deliberately NOT
                            // set -- Configure Endpoint must not touch
                            // the control endpoint.
                            usb_smoke_checkpoint = 95;
                            let icc_drop_ptr: *u32 = input_ctx_base as *u32;
                            *icc_drop_ptr = 0;
                            let icc_add_ptr: *u32 = (input_ctx_base + 4) as *u32;
                            let in_add_bit: u32 = (1 << (in_idx + 1)) as u32;
                            let out_add_bit: u32 = (1 << (out_idx + 1)) as u32;
                            *icc_add_ptr = 1 | in_add_bit | out_add_bit;
                            rpi5_dcache_clean_range(input_ctx_base, INPUT_CTX_SIZE);

                            // Configure Endpoint command (TRB Type 12)
                            // into Command Ring TRB[2].
                            usb_smoke_checkpoint = 96;
                            let ce_dw0_ptr: *u32 = (cmd_ring_base + 32) as *u32;
                            *ce_dw0_ptr = (input_ctx_dma & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
                            let ce_dw1_ptr: *u32 = (cmd_ring_base + 36) as *u32;
                            *ce_dw1_ptr = ((input_ctx_dma >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF) as u32;
                            let ce_dw2_ptr: *u32 = (cmd_ring_base + 40) as *u32;
                            *ce_dw2_ptr = 0;
                            let ce_dw3_ptr: *u32 = (cmd_ring_base + 44) as *u32;
                            *ce_dw3_ptr = (1 | (12 << 10) | (slot_id << 24)) as u32;
                            rpi5_dcache_clean_range(cmd_ring_base, CMD_RING_TRBS * 16);

                            *doorbell0_ptr = 0;
                            let ce_cc: u32 = usb_await_event(33, 1000);
                            uart_puts("configure endpoint: completion_code=");
                            uart_print_hex(ce_cc as i32);
                            uart_puts("\r\n");

                            // Only now tell the DEVICE to switch
                            // configuration. bmRequestType=0x00
                            // (host-to-device, standard, device),
                            // bRequest=9 (SET_CONFIGURATION),
                            // wValue=bConfigurationValue, no data stage.
                            if (ce_cc == 1) {
                                usb_smoke_checkpoint = 97;
                                let setcfg_cc: u32 = usb_ctrl_xfer(
                                    0x00, 9, usb_msc_config_value, 0, 0, 0, false);
                                uart_puts("set configuration: completion_code=");
                                uart_print_hex(setcfg_cc as i32);
                                uart_puts("\r\n");

                                // Slot State must now read 3
                                // (Configured), read back from the
                                // controller's own OUTPUT context.
                                usb_smoke_checkpoint = 98;
                                rpi5_dcache_invalidate_range(device_ctx_base, DEVICE_CTX_SIZE);
                                let cfg_slot_dw3_ptr: *u32 = (device_ctx_base + 12) as *u32;
                                let cfg_slot_dw3: u32 = *cfg_slot_dw3_ptr;
                                uart_puts("device context slot: usb_address=");
                                uart_print_hex((cfg_slot_dw3 & 0xFF) as i32);
                                uart_puts(" slot_state=");
                                uart_print_hex(((cfg_slot_dw3 >> 27) & 0x1F) as i32);
                                uart_puts("\r\n");
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
            } else {
                uart_puts("NO (timed out)\r\n");
                // Diagnostics: USBSTS.HSE (bit2, Host System Error) would
                // indicate a fatal bus error on RP1's own DMA access to
                // host RAM; CRCR.CRR (bit3, Command Ring Running)
                // indicates whether the doorbell ring actually started
                // command processing at all.
                usb_smoke_checkpoint = 66;
                let usbsts_diag_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200024 as *io u32;
                let usbsts_diag: u32 = *usbsts_diag_ptr;
                let crcr_diag_lo_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200038 as *io u32;
                let crcr_diag_lo: u32 = *crcr_diag_lo_ptr;
                let dcbaap_diag_lo_ptr: *io u32 = 0x1F00200050 as *io u32;
                let dcbaap_diag_lo: u32 = *dcbaap_diag_lo_ptr;
                let erdp_diag_lo_ptr: *io u32 = (ir0_base + 0x18) as *io u32;
                let erdp_diag_lo: u32 = *erdp_diag_lo_ptr;
                let erstba_diag_lo_ptr: *io u32 = (ir0_base + 0x10) as *io u32;
                let erstba_diag_lo: u32 = *erstba_diag_lo_ptr;
                uart_puts("diag USBSTS: ");
                uart_print_hex(usbsts_diag as i32);
                uart_puts(" HSE=");
                uart_print(((usbsts_diag >> 2) & 1) as usize);
                uart_puts("\r\ndiag CRCR_lo: ");
                uart_print_hex(crcr_diag_lo as i32);
                uart_puts(" CRR=");
                uart_print(((crcr_diag_lo >> 3) & 1) as usize);
                uart_puts("\r\ndiag DCBAAP_lo (expect ");
                uart_print_hex((dcbaa_dma & 0xFFFFFFFF) as i32);
                uart_puts("): ");
                uart_print_hex(dcbaap_diag_lo as i32);
                uart_puts("\r\ndiag ERDP_lo (expect ");
                uart_print_hex((evt_ring_dma & 0xFFFFFFFF) as i32);
                uart_puts("): ");
                uart_print_hex(erdp_diag_lo as i32);
                uart_puts("\r\ndiag ERSTBA_lo (expect ");
                uart_print_hex((erst_dma & 0xFFFFFFFF) as i32);
                uart_puts("): ");
                uart_print_hex(erstba_diag_lo as i32);
                uart_puts("\r\n");

                // Isolation test (2026-07-26): the fault is now known to
                // trigger specifically between ringing the doorbell and
                // this point (confirmed clean immediately before the
                // doorbell ring). Check whether BCM2712's own PCIe2 link
                // itself is still up -- if the fault took the LINK down
                // entirely (not just a targeted access rejection), that
                // is a materially different, more severe problem.
                let link_still_up: bool = pcie2_link_up();
                uart_puts("PCIe2 link still up after fault: ");
                if (link_still_up) {
                    uart_puts("yes");
                } else {
                    uart_puts("NO");
                }
                uart_puts("\r\n");

                // ERDP's own EHB bit (readback above) came back SET even
                // though software never sets it -- proof the controller
                // DID write at least one event, just not necessarily at
                // evt_ring[0]. Hypothesis: restarting the controller
                // (USBCMD.RS=1) itself queued a Port Status Change Event
                // FIRST (PORTSC's CSC/PEC bits were already pending from
                // way back in Step 2/3, before this example ever armed
                // the Event Ring), pre-empting index 0 before the real
                // Enable Slot completion event could land there. Dump
                // every DWORD of the first 4 event ring entries raw to
                // check.
                rpi5_dcache_invalidate_range(evt_ring_base, 4 * 16);
                for slot: usize in 0..<4 {
                    let base: usize = evt_ring_base + slot * 16;
                    let d0_ptr: *u32 = base as *u32;
                    let d1_ptr: *u32 = (base + 4) as *u32;
                    let d2_ptr: *u32 = (base + 8) as *u32;
                    let d3_ptr: *u32 = (base + 12) as *u32;
                    uart_puts("evt[");
                    uart_print(slot);
                    uart_puts("] dw0=");
                    uart_print_hex((*d0_ptr) as i32);
                    uart_puts(" dw1=");
                    uart_print_hex((*d1_ptr) as i32);
                    uart_puts(" dw2=");
                    uart_print_hex((*d2_ptr) as i32);
                    uart_puts(" dw3=");
                    uart_print_hex((*d3_ptr) as i32);
                    uart_puts("\r\n");
                }
            }
        }
    }

    usb_smoke_checkpoint = 30;
    uart_puts("done\n");
}
